WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:06.590
Film Courage: …So then if you get the okay
on the outline, what is day one of pages like?

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:10.900
Mark Sanderson, screenwriter/author: Day one
of pages is (and I’ll just give you an example

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.740
because last Monday I turned in a new assignment).

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:19.330
So Day 1 of pages is like starting a new job
except it’s at my office.

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:24.570
It’s the same familiar surroundings but
it’s starting a new job and luckily there

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:31.810
wasn’t a great amount of time between me
finishing the treatment and the company saying

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:33.880
“Go!”

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:38.800
Because I like it when it’s fresh still,
that I can jump right on rather this, there’s

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.070
a momentum that’s really important about
things like that.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:46.600
And so I sit down and today is my first day
of writing and I look at my calendar and I

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:53.239
know that I have thirty days and so I do the
math and I divide up the days and say okay

00:00:53.239 --> 00:01:00.230
and I leave myself about 3 or 4 days at the
end of you know I’m not just going to Fade

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:01.739
Out and the end and turn in the script.

00:01:01.739 --> 00:01:05.189
I want to look it over, I want to fix things,
I want to polish it but I don’t want to

00:01:05.189 --> 00:01:08.109
be doing it as I’m writing it because I
can’t do that.

00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:09.729
I have to push forward.

00:01:09.729 --> 00:01:14.299
That’s why a lot of times like the one I
just turned in and I reread it from start

00:01:14.299 --> 00:01:16.759
to finish and I was like “Oh?”

00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:23.209
But I didn’t revisit it for almost a month
ago right because I started to have a feel

00:01:23.209 --> 00:01:27.459
that I didn’t quite remember of this was
good or bad and there are some days when I

00:01:27.459 --> 00:01:31.240
think this is good writing and there’s other
days when I think this is not good.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:37.749
But I always have my back up guide is my treatment
that I’ve worked on there so I’m not just

00:01:37.749 --> 00:01:39.759
left hanging in the wind.

00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:48.279
But I did get to a point and it took a few
days that I had about 5 or 6 page gap in the

00:01:48.279 --> 00:01:53.700
story where I was going to come up short and
I have a mandate about how many pages the

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:58.450
script has to be and I was like how did I
come up short and I find this too sometimes

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:03.279
when I consult on people’s treatments I
say “You don’t have enough story to facilitate

00:02:03.279 --> 00:02:04.329
100 pages here.

00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:09.709
You think you do, but you know they put in
half a page description and I can break that

00:02:09.709 --> 00:02:15.459
down and say in an actual page of script,
that’s going to be about a half of a page.

00:02:15.459 --> 00:02:19.710
You know that’s not, this needs to be four
or five pages of a sequence (you know what

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.920
I mean), so they come up short many times.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:27.269
So I worked though that but like I said I
had my treatment to fall back on and they

00:02:27.269 --> 00:02:34.670
knew these characters so if I just let these
characters run they’re going to follow them,

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:37.670
they’re not going to follow me.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:42.780
I think when you know your characters you
follow them and let them run off and say wait

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:50.769
and say I got it and so that was the first
day and so I was up about three pages a day

00:02:50.769 --> 00:02:54.730
which is a luxury but I’ve also worked on
scripts where I had to do eight to ten pages

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:59.249
a day and I mean if you miss a day and you
can’t miss a day.

00:02:59.249 --> 00:03:04.260
Because there are things in life that happen
and a lot of stuff goes on but it’s a day

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:05.260
job.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:08.750
I mean it’s a day or night job and I’m
lucky that I can push my schedule where my

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:13.569
day can start at 3:00 p.m. and go ’til late.

00:03:13.569 --> 00:03:15.590
I can shift it which is good.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:20.780
A few years ago I was working on an assignment
and that really messed me up because I was

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:25.030
worried more about how I was starting my day
so late and I said why don’t you just relax

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:30.329
and accept the fact that your day starts at
2:00 p.m.

00:03:30.329 --> 00:03:31.599
And I did and you know what?

00:03:31.599 --> 00:03:37.459
It was great once I let go of that I was productive
for the next eight hours.

00:03:37.459 --> 00:03:39.480
So shifting the time, that helps.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:45.749
But again I have (I don’t say the luxury)
but that is my job to do that, to work all

00:03:45.749 --> 00:03:48.689
day on the script and so I better be doing
that.

00:03:48.689 --> 00:03:52.819
Film Courage: I’m sure you trust yourself
more now maybe?

00:03:52.819 --> 00:03:55.249
Because in the beginning maybe it’s too
scary?

00:03:55.249 --> 00:03:57.439
Mark Sanderson: Because you don’t know.

00:03:57.439 --> 00:04:02.200
Without the experience of writing multiple
screenplays and being muddied and in the trenches

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:06.019
and failing, you don’t know your abilities.

00:04:06.019 --> 00:04:14.980
And so I’m not overly-confident, but I know
what I can do, so I say okay and then I met

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:21.310
the deadline for this last job and I had three
or four days left over, so that was a luxury

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:27.430
to be just “Ahhhh” and it felt great the
night before it was due I turned in (I emailed)

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:32.520
the script and so that morning the producer
would get up and sure enough “Here it is,”

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:39.069
just like I said you always have to be building
your integrity constantly.

00:04:39.069 --> 00:04:42.830
They don’t want to have to worry about you.

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:47.770
The one that’s shooting now (a film that
I have shooting in Los Angeles), the producer

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:53.889
is doing other movies at the same time and
he didn’t want to have to worry about me.

00:04:53.889 --> 00:04:58.380
You know, check up all if I’m doing the
job and that’s a good place to be because

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.919
you don’t have to worry about me.

00:05:01.919 --> 00:05:06.669
So you only do that by your experiences.

00:05:06.669 --> 00:05:09.370
When you turn in one job and work with somebody
they go “Okay?”

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:14.310
You turn into another…now that’s just
the norm your integrity in built there which

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:18.970
is also for any screenwriting career if your
professionalism and your integrity.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:23.610
Film Courage: So in the beginning when you
would start a new script once you received

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:28.470
the outline, would you do like 8:00 a.m. and
the coffee’s there and you’re “Okay,

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.389
this is it, the clock’s ticking.”

00:05:30.389 --> 00:05:31.389
Or…

00:05:31.389 --> 00:05:32.389
Mark Sanderson: Aaahh…

00:05:32.389 --> 00:05:36.180
Film Courage: How are you able to say “You
know what, I think I’m better at 2:00 p.m.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:37.180
I know myself.

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:40.889
I know how I need to have lunch first, I need
to do all of these different things, and clear

00:05:40.889 --> 00:05:41.889
my mind.”

00:05:41.889 --> 00:05:47.340
Mark Sanderson: There are breaks but also
the day gets away and that’s not good either.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:49.310
So I don’t have to…8:00 a.m.

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:50.310
(Binngg!)

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:51.789
“Okay, I’ve got to go.”

00:05:51.789 --> 00:05:59.340
But I ease into the morning and then start
work and…I mean three pages a day is a luxury

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:00.810
but eight or ten?

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:02.100
Those are rough days.

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:07.139
And some days you’re not as productive and
you don’t meet that deadline.

00:06:07.139 --> 00:06:09.830
But other days you explode into 12 pages.

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:11.009
How did that happen?

00:06:11.009 --> 00:06:17.750
So it kind of evens out but if you’re doing
fewer pages a day you only have so much time.

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:22.440
That’s what I say when writers are doing
their specs set your own self-imposed deadline.

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:25.509
Don’t let it be open-ended where “Oh,
yeah.

00:06:25.509 --> 00:06:29.479
I was working on this last year and Christmas
came.”

00:06:29.479 --> 00:06:34.639
Because you’re going to need that training
even while you’re working in it with experience,

00:06:34.639 --> 00:06:36.509
it’s still difficult (you’ve got to stand).

00:06:36.509 --> 00:06:40.210
Because you are your own boss, no one is calling
me saying “Are you working today?”

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:42.639
And the day goes on and then…

00:06:42.639 --> 00:06:47.699
You’ve got to really stay on it and realize
that there is a lot of self-work.

00:06:47.699 --> 00:06:49.790
I mean it’s like you are your own boss.

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:52.759
And it’s good in one way people say “I
would love to do that!”

00:06:52.759 --> 00:06:56.610
But with that comes a lot of responsibility
in keeping to that point.

00:06:56.610 --> 00:07:03.129
Film Courage: And with that are you ever saying
to yourself “Okay, now it’s time to turn

00:07:03.129 --> 00:07:04.379
off.”

00:07:04.379 --> 00:07:09.129
Because there is a fear that runs in with
being your own boss where “What if I don’t…”

00:07:09.129 --> 00:07:12.280
Are there different points where you say “I
need to stop now.”

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:18.449
Mark Sanderson: Yes, usually when I feel that
it’s been a good day of writing, there will

00:07:18.449 --> 00:07:26.690
be a point (I forgot which writer said this,
a famous writer) “Leave something more tomorrow

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:29.189
to come back to.”

00:07:29.189 --> 00:07:33.419
And I always look at my characters like they’re
waiting going “Okay, you just had us at

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:34.419
the end of the cliff.

00:07:34.419 --> 00:07:36.550
What are we….aaarghh…we have to wait until
tomorrow.”

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:39.460
Don’t have them jump the cliff and then
go to bed.

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:46.039
So when I would finish a day’s work, it
would feel good to clock out (so to speak)

00:07:46.039 --> 00:07:49.400
at 6:00 p.m. where I go “I’m done!”

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:54.770
But I also knew that if I was behind and in
trouble, I have the rest of the night (whatever

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:56.580
needs to be done).

00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:58.520
But I would prefer not to do that.

00:07:58.520 --> 00:07:59.520
And also weekends, too.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:04.910
A lot of people, screenwriters don’t always
get weekends off and so my friends who have

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:08.810
day jobs I go “You work Monday through Friday.”

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:15.350
I work…sometimes my weekends are Monday
and Tuesday.

00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:21.230
Saturday I have to hit the bricks and do the
work and that’s okay.

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:25.250
But they are like it’s just what happens,
it’s fine.

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:31.680
But the ability to shift the schedule is one
of the things that I enjoy and also the ability

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:34.170
to write anywhere in the world.

00:08:34.170 --> 00:08:38.711
Last year I was in Italy for 6 weeks and I
wrote a job that I got in Los Angeles, so

00:08:38.711 --> 00:08:40.960
the producer didn’t care where I was.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:46.920
We communicated but that was part of the initial
attraction of being of being a writer was

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:51.250
that you didn’t need a big crew and you
didn’t need all these spend two years on

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:58.150
a project that could be in a cafe somewhere
in an Italian island writing a movie.

00:08:58.150 --> 00:08:59.410
Film Courage: Nice.

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:04.440
Mark Sanderson: But bottom line is you have
to write and stick to a schedule, I mean that’s

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:05.440
just vital.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:07.770
Film Courage: That would be very distracting.

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:10.480
Mark Sanderson: It is.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:14.540
I thought it would be…I’m inside and I’m
like “Oh, but there is all this stuff outside

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:15.540
that I should do.”

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:17.290
Film Courage: I want to see The Vatican.

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:21.460
Mark Sanderson: Yeah, but you get over that
when you’re like “Okay, I have to work.”

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:22.760
That helped me to go there so.

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:28.320
I mean it’s true for six weeks so who am
I to complain?

